# DECISIONS — slice S02. Append-only: one line per decision, `date · question · choice · reason · who ruled`.

The mission's gap table and the two `V-ROW: NEW` blocks live in `../S01/DECISIONS.md`; they are not
copied here, because one source or the copies drift.

| date | question | choice | reason | who ruled |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-09-24 | Do (c) the explicit local declaration and (e) the hosted acceptance belong in one slice? | Yes — one slice. | They are the two halves of one question V asked: which deployment does this box boot as. The acceptance exercises both modes in one run, and splitting them would give the smaller half a REV(S) of its own for a two-line change. | REQ-PES (default) |
| 2026-09-24 | Does declaring `DEBATEAI_DEPLOYMENT_MODE=local` locally change the resolution rule? | No — R2.3 pins the rule unchanged and R2.2b adds a case for the declaration. | `packages/register/src/runtime-environment.ts:85` and `tests/unit/v9-deployment-mode.test.ts:264` make "absent outside production is local" a ruled behaviour, not an accident. Overwriting it would break a V-9 case for no gain; the declaration sits on top of it. | REQ-PES (default), within row V-1 |
| 2026-09-24 | A fake vendor on `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/v1`. | REJECTED — hosted mode refuses it by design. | `deploy/vps/README.md:775` refuses the whole `127.0.0.0/8` range and this host's own interface addresses, and `:774` requires `https:`. A loopback fake is exactly what a hosted boot must NOT admit, so it is used as the REFUSAL case (R2.7), never as the admission case. | shipped behaviour (V-9) |
| 2026-09-24 | `/etc/hosts` for the admission case's hostname. | REJECTED in favour of `api.localtest.me` (R2.6). | `sudo` and a mutation of V's machine are outside any acceptance step's contract. The trade is a public-DNS dependency, declared in R2.6, with UNVERIFIED as the honest answer when it cannot resolve. | REQ-PES (default) |
| 2026-09-24 | A plain-HTTP fake endpoint with the TLS check relaxed for the acceptance. | REJECTED. | Relaxing `PROVIDER_BASE_URL_TLS_REQUIRED` for a test would make the acceptance prove a path production does not take, which is the one thing an acceptance may not do. The fixture serves TLS instead (R2.5b). | REQ-PES (default) |
| 2026-09-24 | Reusing the peer session's running relays on `:8791`–`:8796` as the fake vendor. | REJECTED. | COMMON §6 marks them NO-TOUCH; they belong to another session's stack, and a seat that stops or re-points one breaks it. R2.5a requires a port above 4400 the fixture measured free itself. | COMMON §6 |
| 2026-09-24 | Should the acceptance assert on the probe's RESPONSE body? | Only that the target joins the discovered panel and that the fixture saw exactly one matched request (R2.8). | Asserting the body would pin the fake endpoint's own JSON, not the shipped code's behaviour. Panel membership is what `apps/api/src/provider-discovery.ts:63-71` actually decides. | REQ-PES (default) |

## Appended 2026-09-24 by REQ-FIX-PES (node REQ-FIX pass 2 of 3, verdict `../../reviews/REQ-REV-p1.md`) — APPEND-ONLY; nothing above is rewritten

**The SPEC of record for S02 is now `SPEC-v2.md`.** `SPEC.md` stays frozen v1 history.

| date | question | choice | reason | who ruled |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-09-24 | B2: S02's header said its acceptance publishes with S01's command; its own §5 step 5 was the admit line and no requirement named that command. Which reading survives? | The IN-PROCESS reading. The dependency is WITHDRAWN: S02 needs no other slice. | `createProviderDiscoveryResolver` (`apps/api/src/provider-discovery.ts:40-48`) takes `configuredProviders`, `targets`, a probe store, a `fetchImplementation` and a clock as plain arguments — it reads no register and opens no database. So the admission case never needed a published row, and the header was claiming a dependency the steps did not have. Withdrawing it is the smaller change AND makes the slice vertical, which the publishing reading never was (`reviews/REQ-REV-p1.md:23-30`). | REQ-FIX-PES |
| 2026-09-24 | B2, the alternative: keep the dependency, state it in ONE place, mark step 5 UNVERIFIED-until-S01. | REJECTED. | It buys nothing the in-process composition does not already give, and it costs the slice its verticality — V could not run S02's acceptance until S01 merged, and a REV(S) on S02 would have to reason about an unmerged slice. It also kept S02 touching `:55432`, which the in-process reading removes entirely and which is the open N12 question on S01. | REQ-FIX-PES |
| 2026-09-24 | B3: v1's R2.9 forbade the words `Bearer` and `authorization` on any output line, while §5 step 4 required two refusal codes containing `authorization`. Which gives? | R2.9 gives, and is rewritten. A refusal CODE is not a credential. | The secret is the credential VALUE and the credential PATH; a code is a fixed string the shipped source emits (`packages/providers/src/index.ts:306-307`, README:776-778) and hiding it would make the acceptance unreadable for the operator it is written for. v2's R2.9 forbids the literal, a `Bearer `-prefixed token, and the file path, and LISTS the four lawful codes that contain the word. v1's step 7 already conceded the point by excluding `^PES-S02 REFUSED ` from its own grep (`reviews/REQ-REV-p1.md:37`); v2's step 7 greps for the literal and the path instead, so the exclusion is gone. | REQ-FIX-PES |
| 2026-09-24 | N3: what body does the fixture return? | The one the shipped probe accepts, quoted in R2.5c: `model` equal to the target's `model`, and `choices[0].message.content` exactly `OK`. | `packages/providers/src/provider-probe.ts:103` throws `PROVIDER_PROBE_RESPONSE_INVALID` on anything else, which marks the target ABSENT and means the `PES-S02 ADMITTED` line of §5 step 6 never prints. "An OpenAI-shaped body" was a description, not a specification. | REQ-FIX-PES, on `reviews/REQ-REV-p1.md:45` |
| 2026-09-24 | N4: how does the probe trust the fixture's TLS certificate? | A fixture-supplied `fetch` that trusts the fixture's own CA, handed in through the shipped `fetchImplementation` seam (`apps/api/src/provider-discovery.ts:46` → `packages/providers/src/provider-probe.ts:74`). | The seam already exists and is the only injection point the shipped probe offers. `NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0` would disable verification for the whole process, including anything else it touches, and a trust-store edit mutates V's Mac — neither is a thing an acceptance may do. A run that cannot build the trusting `fetch` reports UNVERIFIED rather than falling back to plain HTTP, because a plain-HTTP fake would prove a path hosted mode refuses. | REQ-FIX-PES, on `reviews/REQ-REV-p1.md:47` |
| 2026-09-24 | N5: "by the same means as `DEVELOPMENT_API_ENVIRONMENT_KEYS`" — which record is the runner's? | `apps/runner/src/dev-runner-process.ts:85-128`, a separate object from the API's key list, named explicitly in R2.2. | The runner environment is composed there field by field (`REGISTER_VERSION`, `PROVIDER_REF`, `PROVIDER_DISCOVERY_TARGETS_JSON`, …) and carries no deployment mode today. "The same means" named no place, so two coders would have put the key in two different files. | REQ-FIX-PES, on `reviews/REQ-REV-p1.md:49` |
| 2026-09-24 | N7: §5 step 2 ran "the suite PLAN.md names" and PLAN's cell was empty. | The path is named NOW, by REQ, not deferred to ARCH: `tests/unit/v9-deployment-mode.test.ts`, in both R2.2b and step 2, and in PLAN's R2.2b trace row. | A frozen acceptance that names no file is not runnable, and the two new cases belong in the suite that already holds the mode cases R2.3 pins (`:248`, `:255`, `:264`, `:273`). Deferring it to ARCH would have left the freeze depending on a later node's choice. | REQ-FIX-PES, on `reviews/REQ-REV-p1.md:53` |
| 2026-09-24 | N8: step 9 compared `lsof` PIDs with "the same PIDs they listed before step 3", and nothing recorded them. | A record step is inserted as the new §5 step 3, before the run; the comparison is now step 10. | A comparison against an unrecorded baseline is not a check. This is the same class as B1 — an output whose inputs are not determined — and it is swept with it. | REQ-FIX-PES, on `reviews/REQ-REV-p1.md:55` |

- 2026-09-24 15:08 · **REQ-REV p2 folds (orchestrator).** B2 — step 7 (SPEC-v2 :212-214) greps `<scratch>/credential`, R2.8 (:143-146) makes the scratch dir the 0700 custody dir (`deploy/vps/README.md:788-789`), R2.9 (iii) (:155-158) forbids printing that directory's path, step 5 (:204) and R2.10 (:165-167) require printing it — one layout cannot satisfy both. WHEN: REQ-FIX pass 3 (t_690beb44), SPEC-v3. N1 — R2.8 (:147-150) lists the resolver's arguments without `probeFreshnessMs` and `probeTimeoutMs`, which `createProviderDiscoveryResolver` requires (`apps/api/src/provider-discovery.ts:44-54`; loader default timeout `5_000`, `packages/register/src/runtime-environment.ts:331`); WHEN: same node, both integers written as integers. N2 — :156-157 "any substring of [the literal] after the scheme word" is unsatisfiable on any line containing `e`; step 7 (:213) greps the exact token `pes-s02-fake-vendor-token`; WHEN: same node, the clause names that token.

## Appended 2026-09-24 by REQ-FIX-PES (node REQ-FIX pass 3 of 3, verdict `../../reviews/REQ-REV-p2.md`) — APPEND-ONLY; nothing above is rewritten

**The SPEC of record for S02 is now `SPEC-v3.md`.** `SPEC.md` and `SPEC-v2.md` stay frozen history.
The `SPEC-v2 :NN` pointers in the orchestrator's fold above name v2's defective lines and stay true
of the frozen file; the sentences that replace them are, in `SPEC-v3.md`: R2.7 `:126-150`, R2.8
`:152-177`, R2.9 `:179-187`, R2.10 `:189-195`, step 5 `:232-239`, step 7 `:242-249`.

| date | question | choice | reason | who ruled |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-09-24 | B2: v2's R2.8 made the scratch directory the credential's `0700` custody directory; R2.9 (iii) forbade printing that directory; step 5 and R2.10 required printing the scratch path; step 7 grepped `<scratch>/credential`. Which one layout satisfies all four? | The credential moves one level down, to `<scratch>/custody.d/vendor.header` (directory `0700`, file `0600`). The scratch root is printed; `<scratch>/custody.d` and every path beneath it never is (R2.9 (iii)); step 7 greps `<scratch>/custody.d`, composed from the printed root. | Executed (`probes/REQ-FIX-PES/p3_checks.py`, `check_b2_layout`): the layout passes the shipped `readCustodyAuthorizationHeader`; the `SCRATCH-DIR` line contains neither the file nor its directory; the step-7 pattern is a prefix of the credential's real path, so a line leaking the file or its directory matches it, while the lawful `SCRATCH-DIR` line does not — the grep can fail, which is what the lens required. | REQ-FIX-PES, on `reviews/REQ-REV-p2.md:26-35` (B2) |
| 2026-09-24 | B2, alternative: keep the scratch directory as the custody directory and stop printing its path. | REJECTED. | Step 7 then has nothing to compose its pattern from, and step 9 (the scratch root is gone) has no path to test. | REQ-FIX-PES |
| 2026-09-24 | B2, alternative: keep printing the scratch path and let R2.9 (iii) admit it. | REJECTED. | It admits on stdout the one directory the custody contract keeps private (`deploy/vps/README.md:786-790`). | REQ-FIX-PES |
| 2026-09-24 | B2, alternative: grep for `<scratch>/credential`, or for the file name alone. | REJECTED. | A pattern that is not a prefix of the real path never matches a leak, so the grep prints `0` whether or not the path leaked — the defect the lens found. | REQ-FIX-PES |
| 2026-09-24 | Sweep member, found by execution and not assigned: v2's loopback case named no scheme, and a coder could write `http://127.0.0.1…`. | Every R2.7 fixture is written out byte for byte; the loopback one is `https://127.0.0.1:4455/v1`. | Executed through the shipped chain in `apps/api/src/main.ts:300-318`'s order: the `http:` reading stops one function earlier with `PROVIDER_BASE_URL_TLS_REQUIRED:vendor:a` (`packages/providers/src/index.ts:646`), which is not the refusal the case exists to show; the `https:` reading prints `PROVIDER_TARGET_LOOPBACK_REFUSED:vendor:a` (`:652`). | REQ-FIX-PES |
| 2026-09-24 | Sweep member: which targets does the resolver receive? | The RESOLVED targets `resolveProviderTargetCredentials` returns, as `apps/api/src/main.ts:317-321` passes them. | The parsed targets carry only `authorizationFile`; the probe sends `authorization` only from `authorizationHeader` (`packages/providers/src/provider-probe.ts:71-72`), so a resolver given the parsed targets sends no header and the fake vendor answers 401. | REQ-FIX-PES |
| 2026-09-24 | N1: which `probeFreshnessMs` and `probeTimeoutMs` does the acceptance give the resolver? | `600000` and `5000`, each with its derivation in R2.8's sentence. | `600000` is the `probe_freshness_ms` the development seed already publishes (`apps/runner/src/dev-deployment-register.ts:344`), so no new number is pinned; `5000` is the loader default (`packages/register/src/runtime-environment.ts:331`) the API boots with when the key is absent. Executed: the resolver constructs with both; with neither named, as in v2, it throws `PROVIDER_PROBE_FRESHNESS_INVALID` (`apps/api/src/provider-discovery.ts:49-54`). | REQ-FIX-PES, on `reviews/REQ-REV-p2.md` N1 |
| 2026-09-24 | N2: R2.9 (i) forbade "any substring" of the credential literal after the scheme word. | Clause (i) forbids the exact token `pes-s02-fake-vendor-token` — the token step 7 greps for. | Every single character is a substring, so v2's clause forbade every line the acceptance must print, `PES-S02 SCRATCH-DIR` included (executed, `check_n2_stdout_rule`). | REQ-FIX-PES, on `reviews/REQ-REV-p2.md` N2 |
| 2026-09-24 | Sweep member, found by execution: v2's step 5 required every refusal code "followed by the provider ref it refused". | Step 5 now says a ref follows each code that ends in `:`. | The shipped `PROVIDER_DISCOVERY_AUTHORIZATION_CONFLICT` carries no ref (`packages/providers/src/index.ts:307`; executed: the `refused-conflict` fixture prints exactly that code), so v2's step 5 could never hold for that line. | REQ-FIX-PES |

## Appended 2026-09-25 by ARCH-PES-S02 (node ARCH(S02) pass 1 of 3, ticket t_b4187218) — APPEND-ONLY; nothing above is rewritten

These rows record the architecture of `PLAN.md` for `SPEC-v3.md`, each alternative that was rejected, and why. Anchors are
`path:LINE` in the lane `.worktrees/pes-s02/dialectical-engine` at `776359c3`, re-grepped on 2026-09-25. Probe evidence lives in
`.hermes/reports/provider-env-selection/probes/ARCH-PES-S02/`. Each contested choice carries VERDICT / CONFIDENCE /
STRONGEST COUNTER (COMMON §4). Brainstorming's "explicit yes" is discharged by this record (packet charge 6).

| date | question | choice | reason | who ruled |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-09-25 | Where does `DEBATEAI_DEPLOYMENT_MODE` sit in `DEVELOPMENT_API_ENVIRONMENT_KEYS` (`apps/runner/src/dev-api-environment.ts:36-78`)? | LAST, after `"DEBATEAI_DEV_MAIL_CAPTURE_DIR"` (`:77`); its `values` entry is last too (after `:529`). | `api.env` is written in list order (`environmentSource`, `:248-256`) and read back by index (`apps/runner/src/dev-api-process.ts:131-146`). Appending makes a pre-slice file a strict PREFIX of the new one, so the upgrade of the next row is a pure suffix and composes with the four existing acceptance predicates unchanged. REJECTED: beside `NODE_ENV` (`:68`). That puts a pre-slice file's difference mid-file and needs a positional splice. VERDICT last / CONFIDENCE high / STRONGEST COUNTER: grouping by meaning (mode beside `NODE_ENV`) is the order a reader of the file V opens would expect. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | What happens on V's machine, whose custody root already holds an `api.env` assembled before this slice? | A fifth acceptance predicate (PLAN S02-S06). A file that lacks the `DEBATEAI_DEPLOYMENT_MODE=` row and ends in `\n` is accepted and rewritten when the file WITH `DEBATEAI_DEPLOYMENT_MODE=local\n` appended passes equality or one of the four shipped predicates (`:325-448`). | Without it, `publishExactFile` throws `DEV_API_ENVIRONMENT_DRIFT` (`:284-287`) on V's next `dev:auth:up`, so the slice would stop the local stack whose declaration it adds. R2.1 says the dev stack ASSEMBLES the key, and on V's machine it cannot assemble without this. House precedent: `:409-419` (legacy probe timeout) and `:421-448` (legacy Support target). REJECTED: (a) no predicate, which gives DRIFT on V's stack; (b) accepting any file that lacks the row, which admits drift in every other field (PLAN S02-S03 (b) kills this mutant); (c) telling V to delete `api.env` by hand, a manual step on V's machine the house pattern avoids. VERDICT predicate / CONFIDENCE high / STRONGEST COUNTER: no SPEC sentence names the upgrade, so this is code the SPEC did not ask for. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | Does the dev API launcher pin the declared value? | Yes. `["DEBATEAI_DEPLOYMENT_MODE", "local"]` is appended as the last entry of the `exact` map in `validateExactEnvironment` (`apps/runner/src/dev-api-process.ts:181-214`, after `:213`). | Every non-secret value the assembler writes is already pinned there (`:182-213`). The shipped `parseApiEnvironment` (`:171`) accepts `hosted`, so without the pin a hand-edited dev `api.env` boots the dev API hosted. It then fails LATER, at `PROVIDER_TARGET_LOOPBACK_REFUSED`, on the local relays. REJECTED: no pin. VERDICT pin / CONFIDENCE medium / STRONGEST COUNTER: SPEC §2 says the slice adds a declaration and "no decision point". The pin decides nothing about the mode, which the loader still resolves; it refuses a file that is not what the assembler wrote, the launcher's existing job. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | How does the runner record declare the mode (R2.2)? | The literal `DEBATEAI_DEPLOYMENT_MODE: "local"` in `createRunnerEnvironment`'s record, after `RUNNER_WORKER_ID` (`apps/runner/src/dev-runner-process.ts:85`). | The module is development-only, and it already declares its constants as literals (`:85`, `:93-101`, `:114-115`). REJECTED: (a) forwarding `apiEnvironment.DEBATEAI_DEPLOYMENT_MODE`, which makes the runner's declaration a copy of a file read at run time; with a record lacking the key (the fixture at `tests/unit/dev-runner-process.test.ts:19-36`) it is `undefined`. (b) One exported constant shared by both records, a new binding for a two-character value, where R2.2 names two SEPARATE objects. VERDICT literal / CONFIDENCE medium / STRONGEST COUNTER: two literal sites can drift apart. The two v9 cases (PLAN S02-S02) pin both values. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | Where does R2.1's v9 case get an assembler it can run? | A new support file `tests/support/devApiEnvironmentAssembly.ts`, a copy of the DEV-09 fixture (`tests/integration/dev-api-environment.test.ts:42-102`). | "The case asserts the assembled value" (R2.2b), and the only assembler, `assembleDevelopmentApiEnvironment` (`:450-542`), needs a private custody tree, credential files and a register receipt. REJECTED: (a) inlining it in the v9 suite, about 60 lines of custody fixture in a mode suite; (b) moving the integration suite's fixture into `tests/support` and importing it from both, which edits a RED-at-base suite R2.4 records case by case, for no requirement. VERDICT copy / CONFIDENCE medium / STRONGEST COUNTER: two copies of a ~50-line fixture drift. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | Can R2.1's v9 case assemble into a real custody root? | No. It stubs `DEBATEAI_DEV_CUSTODY_ROOT` to the fixture's own `<root>/.local/dev-auth` and unstubs after each case. | `resolveDevCustodyRoot` honours that variable (`deploy/dev-auth/custody-root.mjs:97-101`). Without the stub, a shell that exports it makes the case assemble into that root. The DEV-09 siblings do not stub it; that is reported as a finding, not fixed here (out of the slice's file surface). | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | How does the acceptance run "the chain the shipped API composition root takes, in its order" (R2.7)? | It calls the four shipped functions in `apps/api/src/main.ts:299-318`'s order: `parseProviderDiscoveryTargets`, then `assertDeploymentProviderTargets(declared, {mode, nodeEnv})`, then `assertPricedProviderTargets(declared, mode)`, then `resolveProviderTargetCredentials(declared, readCustodyAuthorizationHeader)`. They are imported from `../apps/api/src/provider-discovery.js`, the module `main.ts:69-75` imports them from (it re-exports the four at `:6-16`). `readCustodyAuthorizationHeader` comes from `@debateai/crypto` (`main.ts:12-18`), and `mode = resolveDeploymentMode("hosted", "production")` from `@debateai/register` (`packages/register/src/index.ts:792`). | Measured at base (`probes/ARCH-PES-S02/spike-hosted-chain.log`): the five fixtures produce SPEC-v3's five messages exactly, at stages parse (conflict), deployment (loopback, inline), priced (price) and credentials (absent). REJECTED: extracting the chain from `main.ts` into an exported function both call, a refactor of the API boot path that no requirement asks for. VERDICT call in order / CONFIDENCE medium / STRONGEST COUNTER: a later reorder of `main.ts` would leave the acceptance proving the old order (PLAN §8 Review Focus #2). | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | Which `nodeEnv` does the acceptance give `assertDeploymentProviderTargets`? | `"production"`. | The VPS example boots `NODE_ENV=production` (`deploy/vps/env/api.env.example:9`). Measured: the five messages are identical under `"production"` and `undefined` (spike log). | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | How is the fixture's TLS certificate generated at run time (R2.5b)? | `/usr/bin/openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -days 1 -config <scratch>/tls/openssl.cnf`, writing `key.pem` and `cert.pem` into `<scratch>/tls`. ONE self-signed certificate (`basicConstraints=critical,CA:TRUE`, `subjectAltName=DNS:api.localtest.me`) is both the server certificate and the fetch's only trust anchor. | Measured at base with both binaries: `/usr/bin/openssl` is LibreSSL 3.3.6 (37 ms) and `openssl` on the acceptance PATH is Homebrew OpenSSL 3.6.4 (93 ms). Node 26 accepts the certificate through the seam, and `~/.rnd` stays absent. REJECTED: (a) `mkcert`, the house tool (`deploy/dev-auth/create-local-certificate.mjs:115-136`): its CA lives in `CAROOT` outside the scratch root (R2.10) and `-install` edits the trust store (R2.5b). (b) A committed certificate and key: R2.5b says generated at run time, and it puts a private key in the tree. (c) `openssl` from PATH, because PATH order decides which binary runs; the absolute system binary does not depend on it. (d) A separate CA and leaf: two invocations, with no check the single anchor misses. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | How is the trusting `fetch` built (R2.5b)? | `node:https.request` through a dedicated `new https.Agent({ ca: [certPem], keepAlive: false })` with `rejectUnauthorized: true`, returning a real `Response`. It is handed to `createProviderDiscoveryResolver` as `fetchImplementation`. | The probe asks `fetch` for a string URL, `POST`, a header record, `AbortSignal.timeout`, a string body, `.ok` and `.text()` (`packages/providers/src/provider-probe.ts:74-91`). Measured: it admits `vendor:a` through the seam, while the default `fetch` refuses the same server with `DEPTH_ZERO_SELF_SIGNED_CERT`. REJECTED: (a) an `undici` `Agent`, not a dependency (`node_modules/undici` absent; adding it needs an install and a lockfile change); (b) `NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED` or `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`, forbidden by R2.5b. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | What shape does the acceptance take? | A module `acceptance/pes-s02-hosted.ts` exporting `runHostedAcceptance(deps)`, a thin CLI `acceptance/pes-s02-hosted-cli.ts`, and a fixture module `acceptance/pes-s02-fake-vendor.ts`, each with a suite beside it. | House pattern: `acceptance/**/*.test.ts` is in vitest's include (`vitest.config.ts:17`), and `acceptance/eval-harness-cli.ts` is a CLI over a module. Step 8's FAIL sentence and R2.5b/R2.6's UNVERIFIED reports must be exercised, which needs seams. REJECTED: (a) one script with no seams, where those paths could only be reached by editing the script; (b) environment-variable fault hooks, an env switch that changes what the acceptance proves. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | Do the suites depend on public DNS? | No. The suites inject a `lookup` answering `api.localtest.me` with `127.0.0.1`; the acceptance never does, and uses the OS resolver. | The acceptance keeps R2.6's public-DNS dependency and its UNVERIFIED report; the suites stay runnable offline. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | What does a PASS run print? | EXACTLY 13 lines (PLAN §6, S02-S18): the SPEC's lines plus three evidence lines, `PES-S02 DNS`, `PES-S02 PORT-FREE` and `PES-S02 TRUST`. | PORT-FREE is this PLAN's own §4 item ("the `lsof` line that showed it free BEFORE the bind"). DNS is R2.6's "resolves to this Mac" as observed. TRUST shows R2.5b's "never through the environment" at run time: a handshake through the seam is authorized AND the default `fetch` is refused. REJECTED: exactly the SPEC's lines, which leaves those three claims unobservable in V's log. VERDICT 13 lines / CONFIDENCE medium / STRONGEST COUNTER: step 5 lists its lines "in this order". The extra lines sit between named lines and never reorder them. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | Does a failing run go on after the first case that does not hold? | No. It stops, tears down, and names that case. | Step 8 names only "the first case that did not hold". REJECTED: evaluating every case. | ARCH-PES-S02 |
| 2026-09-25 | Which port does the fake vendor take (R2.5a)? | The first of 4460–4499 that is not in R2.5a's list and not 4455, for which `lsof -nP -iTCP:<port> -sTCP:LISTEN` exits 1 with no output. It is bound on `127.0.0.1` only. | 4455 appears in every R2.7 fixture, which R2.7 says is "never bound and never connected to". Measured at base: 4460 was free. REJECTED: port 0, because the OS then picks a port the run did not measure before binding (R2.5a). | ARCH-PES-S02 |
